9) Approval overlay interaction contracts (ui/approval_overlay.py)
   Location: tuan_cli/ui/approval_overlay.py
             (consumed by sandbox/isolation.py + sandbox/manager.py for E7,
              and tools/mcp/policy.py for E1)
   Origin:   interaction contracts and component layering of the Codex TUI
             approval overlay, specifically
             `codex-rs/tui/src/bottom_pane/approval_overlay.rs`,
             `list_selection_view.rs`, `popup_consts.rs`
   Upstream: https://github.com/openai/codex
   Holder:   OpenAI (Copyright 2025 OpenAI)
   License:  Apache License 2.0 (full text: THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/Codex-LICENSE.txt;
             upstream NOTICE reproduced verbatim in
             THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/Codex-NOTICE.txt, which additionally
             acknowledges code derived from Ratatui under the MIT license,
             Copyright (c) 2016-2022 Florian Dehau and
             Copyright (c) 2023-2025 The Ratatui Developers)

   Note: the upstream `codex-tui` crate is Rust + ratatui + crossterm + tokio
   (1216 .rs files, 492414 lines; 165 top-level modules; 1491 snapshot files
   skipped). Its source was downloaded and inspected in
   full for this attribution. NO Rust source code is copied or ported
   verbatim into Tuan-CLI: the project is pure Python with prompt_toolkit,
   and replacing the TUI shell wholesale would also replace the entry point
   that carries the permission-policy injection, the confirmation channel
   and the sandbox routing (i.e. it would re-open the side doors fixed in
   the E1-E8 hardening series). What is reproduced is the *interaction
   contract* only, independently implemented in Python:

     1. a selection always emits an explicit decision event (no implicit
        "absence of input means consent");
     2. Esc / cancel always maps to Cancel/Abort, never to "continue
        without info" even with custom keybindings;
     3. the UI never evaluates whether an action is safe — it presents the
        choices and routes the decision (upstream wording: "This module
        does not evaluate whether an action is safe to run; it only
        presents choices and routes user decisions.");
     4. turn-scope vs session-scope grants are distinguished.

   The card layout follows Tuan-CLI's existing box renderer (the same one
   used by `_render_confirm_card` / `_render_ask_card`), not ratatui's
   Widget/Buffer traits. Modifications relative to upstream: the request
   model is a Python dataclass; decision parsing is fail-closed (unknown
   input -> RETRY, non-TTY headless -> an explicit 6-char confirmation
   code, EOF/unavailable stdin -> ABORT); E1 adds a persistent MCP trust
   grant flow and E7 adds a structured DiffPreview card, neither of which
   exists upstream. OpenAI does not endorse or sponsor this project.

10) TUI parity/alignment layer (tuan_cli/tui/parity/, cli/cmd_tui_parity.py)
    Location: tuan_cli/tui/parity/{upstream_spec,keymap,i18n,matrix}.py
              tuan_cli/cli/cmd_tui_parity.py, TUI_PARITY.md
    Origin:   functional facts extracted from the Codex TUI source:
              `lib.rs` (165 top-level module names),
              `keymap.rs::built_in_defaults` (key bindings),
              `config/src/tui_keymap.rs` (key-spec normalization rules),
              `ui_consts.rs` / `bottom_pane/popup_consts.rs` /
              `list_selection_view.rs` (layout constants),
              `bottom_pane/approval_overlay.rs` / `popup_consts.rs` /
              `ui_consts.rs` (English UI strings for localization)
    Upstream: https://github.com/openai/codex
    Holder:   OpenAI (Copyright 2025 OpenAI)
    License:  Apache License 2.0 (same files as component 9)

    Note: this layer records **verifiable functional facts** (module names,
    numeric layout constants, key bindings, normalization rules) and
    independently implements them in Python. NO Rust source is copied.
    English UI strings are listed as source material only and are rendered
    through Tuan-CLI's own 10-language localization; they are not shipped
    as upstream expressive content. Modifications: component tree is
    regrouped into Tuan functional domains; keymap resolution adds explicit
    fail-closed behavior; `Esc` maps to ABORT (stricter than upstream's
    exec-mode decline); `approve_for_prefix` is localized to an in-session
    grant and flagged as such; E1 session-scope grants are persisted to
    disk (a wider scope than upstream, disclosed on the card). See
    TUI_PARITY.md sections 3, 6 and 8 for the full change list.

